      {
        "narration": "Priya is making granola. Her scoop holds exactly 1/3 cup of oats. That is a unit fraction, because the numerator is 1. The denominator, 3, tells us three scoops make one whole cup.",
        "text": "1/3 cup",
        "visual": { "kind": "emoji", "emoji": "🥣", "count": null, "filled": null }
      },
      {
        "narration": "Priya fills the scoop 4 times. That is 4 groups of 1/3. We can add them: 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3.",
        "text": "4 × 1/3 = 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3",
        "visual": { "kind": "objects", "emoji": "🥄", "count": 4, "filled": null }
      },
      {
        "narration": "Add the numerators: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 is 4. The denominator stays 3, because every piece is still a third. So 4 × 1/3 equals 4/3, or 1 1/3 cups.",
        "text": "4 × 1/3 = 4/3",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "narration": "Now flip the question. Priya wants to fill 4 whole cups using the same scoop. How many scoops of 1/3 fit in 4 wholes? Start with one cup. It takes 3 scoops.",
        "text": "1 cup = 3 scoops",
        "visual": { "kind": "objects", "emoji": "🥄", "count": 3, "filled": null }
      },
      {
        "narration": "Four cups need four times as many scoops. That is 4 × 3, which is 12. So 4 ÷ 1/3 equals 12.",
        "text": "4 ÷ 1/3 = 12",
        "visual": { "kind": "objects", "emoji": "🥄", "count": 12, "filled": null }
      },
      {
        "narration": "Here is the pattern. Multiplying a whole number by 1/3 gives that many thirds. Dividing a whole number by 1/3 means multiplying by the denominator, 3. Ready to practise?",
        "text": "n × 1/3 = n/3   n ÷ 1/3 = n × 3",
        "visual": null
      }
